WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
And we'll start on page 35, lab values. You have to know your lab values.

2
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.000
But the good news is you don't have to know them all.

3
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:17.000
Just certain ones that everybody else knows.

4
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.000
The only...

5
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.000
Well, there's kind of more bad news.

6
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:26.000
And that is, not only do you have to know lab values,

7
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:33.000
but you have to know which lab values are more dangerous than what other lab values.

8
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:38.000
So if you knew 10 lab values, you need to know which ones are high priority,

9
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.000
which ones are middle priority, and which ones are low priority.

10
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:46.000
Because they will ask you to prioritize people according to their lab values.

11
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Like they'll give you four different patients.

12
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.000
A patient with a potassium of 7.4, potassium with a pH of 6.8,

13
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:59.000
a person with a hemoglobin of 10, and a person with a BUN of 54,

14
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.000
and they want to know who's your highest priority patient.

15
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.000
That kind of thing.

16
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:09.000
So it's not good enough just to know the values themselves.

17
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:14.000
Because they will give you four people, all of them have abnormal lab values.

18
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.000
All of them do.

19
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.000
So what you have to do is then rank them.

20
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.000
You know, on whose highest, whose lowest priority.

21
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:33.000
So what I want to do is teach you that because I don't believe any school I know of

22
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:37.000
teaches students how to prioritize lab values.

23
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:39.000
They teach you the lab values.

24
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.000
They teach you the numbers, the ranges.

25
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.000
What's normal, what's abnormal.

26
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:46.000
They teach you what high means, what low means.

27
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.000
They teach you the significance of the test.

28
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.000
But they don't teach you that a hemoglobin of 7 is a higher priority

29
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:56.000
than a person with an INR of 3.4.

30
00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:57.000
Do you see what I'm saying?

31
00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.000
They don't get to that.

32
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:00.000
Am I mistaken?

33
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.000
Are some schools doing that?

34
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.000
Are any schools doing that?

35
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.000
I'm just saying potassium is bad.

36
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.000
They just say potassium is bad.

37
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:15.000
And that's probably, probably on boards that'd probably be the least you should know,

38
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:16.000
at least that.

39
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:19.000
But, well I'm going to give you a scheme here.

40
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:24.000
You see the legend at the top of the page where it says ABCD.

41
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.000
A means that it's not a priority.

42
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:28.000
I mean it's a low priority.

43
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:29.000
It's not a big deal.

44
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.000
Yes, it's abnormal.

45
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:33.000
The lab is abnormal.

46
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:36.000
But really there's nothing you need to do about it.

47
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:42.000
In fact, in real life, if, see what I'm going to do is, let me back up.

48
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:46.000
What I'm going to do is, I'm going to, for every lab value I give you,

49
00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:51.000
I'll tell you whether the abnormal is an A, a B, a C, or a D.

50
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:54.000
I'll assign a priority letter to it.

51
00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.000
Are you understanding where we're headed with this?

52
00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:01.000
And when I tell you that a lab abnormality is a level A,

53
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:06.000
what I'm trying to communicate to you is that, yes, it is abnormal.

54
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:10.000
And yes, it could mean there is the presence of disease.

55
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.000
But in the scheme of things, this is really no big deal.

56
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:17.000
In fact, you don't have to do anything about it.

57
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:25.000
In other words, if a lab report came back and it's an A level,

58
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:31.000
you could ignore it all night long and have the doctor discover it in the morning

59
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:36.000
and you wouldn't be in any trouble for having that happen.

60
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:37.000
Do you understand what I'm saying?

61
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:41.000
However, if I tell you it's a C level,

62
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:47.000
level C, letter C, and you do nothing about it all night long

63
00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:53.000
and the doctor finds out about it in the morning, you are in major trouble.

64
00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:57.000
So I'm trying to give you a feel for how important it is

65
00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:00.000
that you must report these or do something immediately.

66
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.000
So when A is abnormal, yes, but you do nothing about it, it's no big deal.

67
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:08.000
A letter B, it is abnormal and you need to be concerned,

68
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:10.000
but there's nothing you need to do.

69
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:12.000
You just kind of watch them closer.

70
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:16.000
Have you had the experience where you had a lab value that was abnormal

71
00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:18.000
but it really wasn't all that bad?

72
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:22.000
So you didn't have to do anything, but that lab abnormality said to you,

73
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:24.000
watch them closer, watch them closer.

74
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:28.000
That's what I'm trying to tell you a B is.

75
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:30.000
When I tell you that a lab value is a C,

76
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.000
we've now crossed the line from low priority to high priority.

77
00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:39.000
When a lab reaches a C value, it is critical.

78
00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:41.000
You must do something about it.

79
00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:44.000
You can't just sit on that lab value and do nothing.

80
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:47.000
If I tell you that a lab abnormality is a D,

81
00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:50.000
I'm trying to communicate to you that it is the highest priority

82
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:54.000
that you can possibly have with a lab value.

83
00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:56.000
So what's the lowest priority?

84
00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:59.000
Followed by B.

85
00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:01.000
And then what are two high priorities?

86
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:02.000
B.

87
00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:04.000
And the highest of all is D.

88
00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:06.000
Just so everybody knows where we're headed here.

89
00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:09.000
Now, let's start into this lecture.

90
00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:13.000
In the first column, there's the name and anything I want to tell you about it.

91
00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:17.000
In the middle column, you'll write the range, the normal range.

92
00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:21.000
And in the last column, we'll assign the A, the B, the C, or the D.

93
00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:23.000
Let's talk about pre-atinent.

94
00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:31.000
I told you two days ago that it is the best indicator of kidney or renal function.

95
00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:34.000
Now, in this case, I am talking about the serum creatinine

96
00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:42.000
because they will not give you the values for a creatinine clearance,

97
00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:44.000
24-hour creatinine clearance.

98
00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:46.000
They are going to test you on that.

99
00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:49.000
But they will test you on the serum creatinine.

100
00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:58.000
Now, the serum creatinine is 0.6 to 1.2,

101
00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:03.000
which is actually the same numbers as the lithium range.

102
00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:09.000
If you recall that from yesterday, the lithium and the creatinine are the same range.

103
00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:12.000
Okay, in the middle column, you have that.

104
00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:18.000
In the last column, an abnormal creatinine is just a level A.

105
00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:24.000
You would never prioritize a person with a high creatinine as your highest priority.

106
00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:27.000
Now, do they have kidney disease?

107
00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:30.000
If they have a high cred, yes.

108
00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:34.000
But are they going to die in the next four hours? No.

109
00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:36.000
The next morning, the doctor could come in and go,

110
00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:41.000
oh, his creatinine is high. He must have kidney disease, and that's fine.

111
00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:44.000
You're not going to be in any trouble for it.

112
00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:49.000
Probably the only time that I would ever make a high creatinine something

113
00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:54.000
that I would actually make a phone call to a doctor about

114
00:06:54.000 --> 00:07:04.000
is if they were going for a test that had a die in it that next morning.

115
00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:09.000
Like, for example, if they're having a cardiac catheterization where they put the die in.

116
00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:16.000
If the question tells you that they're going to have a die procedure in the morning,

117
00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:21.000
I might make a high creatinine something that I would report.

118
00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:25.000
But it wouldn't be in the next hour. It would just be sometime, like, for example,

119
00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:28.000
if you got the high creatinine back at midnight,

120
00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:33.000
I wouldn't call the doctor until five or six in the morning at the earliest.

121
00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:35.000
I might wait until seven.

122
00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:38.000
I certainly wouldn't call him a one in the morning.

123
00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:42.000
But there are certain lab values you're going to see. You call them right away.

124
00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:46.000
So this would even then, it wouldn't be a super high priority,

125
00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:50.000
but it would be higher than just a plain high creatinine.

126
00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:52.000
Does everybody understand that?

127
00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:54.000
Okay.

128
00:07:54.000 --> 00:08:01.000
The INR, the International Normalized Ratio, it monitors Coumadin therapy.

129
00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:05.000
It's like the PT.

130
00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:09.000
It's a variation of the prothrombin time.

131
00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:15.000
Its normal range should be two and three, in the twos and threes.

132
00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:20.000
In other words, you want your INRs to be in the twos and the threes.

133
00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:26.000
Like 2.1, 3.8, that's what you want.

134
00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:35.000
In the last column, anything that's above four, or four and above, is the C,

135
00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:38.000
which means it's high priority.

136
00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:41.000
You have to do something.

137
00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:43.000
This is not one you can ignore.

138
00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:49.000
You can ignore the creatinine, but you cannot ignore this one.

139
00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:57.000
An INR of 4.2 would defeat, would be a higher priority than a creatinine of 30.

140
00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:09.000
Now, when I assign something a letter C, what do I say are the implications of that?

141
00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:11.000
It's critical and you have to what?

142
00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:13.000
You do something.

143
00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:17.000
Now, the question you should be asking is, well, what's the do that I should do?

144
00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:25.000
Well, whenever you get a situation where they want to know what are you going to do about something,

145
00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:32.000
there's a protocol you need to follow, an order, because this would be a drag, a click and drag.

146
00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:35.000
You always hold.

147
00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:38.000
That's the first thing you do.

148
00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:42.000
What do I mean by hold?

149
00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:46.000
If there's something that's causing a problem, stop it first.

150
00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:47.000
First.

151
00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:54.000
After you hold, then you assess.

152
00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:57.000
What would you assess?

153
00:09:57.000 --> 00:10:02.000
A whole, complete, head-to-toe physical exam, yes or no?

154
00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:07.000
No, a focused assessment.

155
00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:09.000
Focused on what?

156
00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:15.000
The area that the lab value is telling you there's no problem with, but through a focused assessment.

157
00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:27.000
Then you prepare, which means you prepare to give.

158
00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:32.000
Now, you don't always give, but you prepare to.

159
00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:43.000
And after you prepare, then you call, meaning call position or call respiratory or call whomever is appropriate.

160
00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:50.000
But before you call the physician, you always hold, assess and prepare.

161
00:10:53.000 --> 00:11:01.000
Now, if we apply this to the INR of 4.7, let's say you get an INR of 4.7,

162
00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:07.000
and they want to know what are you going to do in what order and you have to click and drag.

163
00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:11.000
What would you click first?

164
00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:13.000
Hold what?

165
00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:15.000
Bold Coumadin.

166
00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:16.000
Bold All Coumadin.

167
00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:18.000
Then what would you do?

168
00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:20.000
Assess for what?

169
00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:22.000
Bleeding.

170
00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:24.000
Then you would?

171
00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:25.000
Vitamin K.

172
00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:29.000
Prepare to give Vitamin K, and then you would?

173
00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:31.000
Call your doctor.

174
00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:33.000
In that order.

175
00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:40.000
Now, sometimes there may be nothing to hold.

176
00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:41.000
You understand what I'm saying?

177
00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:42.000
There's nothing to hold.

178
00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:46.000
There's nothing causing a problem, so you jump immediately to what?

179
00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:48.000
Assess.

180
00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:53.000
And sometimes there may not be anything to prepare, so you jump to call.

181
00:11:53.000 --> 00:12:03.000
You should intellectually go through that process with everything you do something about so that you're thorough, so that you don't miss a step.

182
00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:09.000
Potassium.

183
00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:16.000
Potassium, there's nothing I want to say about it in the first column, except that it's not really a good indicator of anything,

184
00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:20.000
but it is an indicator that something's wrong, but you don't know why.

185
00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:28.000
Its range is 3.5, and then flip it around 5.3.

186
00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:30.000
3.5 to 5.3.

187
00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:36.000
Now, for HECI, it's 3.5 to 5.0.

188
00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:39.000
For HECI, it's 3.5 to 5.0.

189
00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:42.000
But for BORDS, it's 3.5 to 5.3.

190
00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:43.000
Why?

191
00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:45.000
Why the difference?

192
00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:52.000
BORDS is a nationalized HECI uses whatever Elsevier Labbook says.

193
00:12:52.000 --> 00:13:02.000
So, to HECI, a 5.1 potassium is an elevation. On BORDS a 5.1 is not an elevation.

194
00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:07.000
Now, let's talk about the last column.

195
00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:14.000
A low potassium, meaning lower than 3.5, is a C. You have to do something.

196
00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:17.000
Okay, now, let's go through our protocol.

197
00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:21.000
If your potassium is low, is there anything you have to hold?

198
00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:24.000
No.

199
00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:26.000
So, what do you do?

200
00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:29.000
What are you assessing? What's your focus?

201
00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:30.000
Heart.

202
00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:32.000
Assess heart?

203
00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:37.000
Prepare to administer vitamin K.

204
00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:38.000
Vitamin K.

205
00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:41.000
Potassium. I always do that. I always used to do that.

206
00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:43.000
Anybody ever do that, vitamin K and potassium K?

207
00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:46.000
I screwed that up for like 10 years.

208
00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:48.000
Okay, and then I what?

209
00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:49.000
Called the duck.

210
00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:52.000
All right.

211
00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:57.000
What if it's between 5.4 and 5.9?

212
00:13:57.000 --> 00:13:59.000
That gets a C.

213
00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:02.000
So, now, you have to do something.

214
00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:04.000
Well, is it high or low?

215
00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:05.000
High.

216
00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:07.000
So, what do you think we would first do?

217
00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:09.000
Hold all potassium.

218
00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:15.000
And that may mean ripping down the D5W with 20 of KCL.

219
00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:17.000
That means getting that torn down.

220
00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:20.000
Then you assess the heart.

221
00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:21.000
Heart.

222
00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:23.000
Then you...

223
00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:26.000
What?

224
00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:27.000
What?

225
00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:36.000
Now, LASIX does make you lose potassium, but we don't give LASIX for the purpose of losing potassium.

226
00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:41.000
K-exilade and D5W and regular insulin. Remember yesterday?

227
00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:44.000
K-exilade and D5W and regular insulin.

228
00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:47.000
And then you call your duck.

229
00:14:47.000 --> 00:15:00.000
However, in the last column, if the potassium is greater than or equal to 6, it's a D.

230
00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:03.000
It is deadly dangerous.

231
00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:08.000
This person could die soon, like in the next two minutes.

232
00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:11.000
So, what do you do?

233
00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:13.000
You do everything we just said.

234
00:15:13.000 --> 00:15:20.000
You rip down the IV, you assess the heart, you prepare the D5W, regular insulin and the K-exilade,

235
00:15:20.000 --> 00:15:23.000
and you call your duck, and you do all that what?

236
00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:24.000
Stats.

237
00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:27.000
So, how many people have to be involved?

238
00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:30.000
You probably got one nurse ripping out the what?

239
00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:31.000
Potassium.

240
00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:35.000
You got somebody calling for an EKG coming.

241
00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:41.000
One nurse is preparing the D5W in regular insulin while the secretary's calling the doctor's stat.

242
00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:45.000
And you stay with your patient.

243
00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:47.000
Got it?

244
00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:53.000
Because you cannot leave the, you can't leave the bedside of a D.

245
00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:54.000
You understand what I'm saying?

246
00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:58.000
Can you leave the bedside of a C?

247
00:15:58.000 --> 00:16:00.000
Do you even know what I'm talking about? D's and C's.

248
00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:01.000
Yeah.

249
00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:02.000
Okay, got it. Okay.

250
00:16:02.000 --> 00:16:05.000
Can you leave the bedside of a C?

251
00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:06.000
Yeah.

252
00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:08.000
Can you leave the bedside of a D?

253
00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:09.000
No.

254
00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:14.000
One of the things you're going to have to remember when you're answering these D questions is you stay at the bedside,

255
00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:19.000
and you do what you can do at the bedside, but everybody else helps you do everything else.

256
00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:25.000
You don't run out and get the EKG and leave your patient by themselves with a potassium of 7, okay?

257
00:16:25.000 --> 00:16:29.000
Now, I'm not saying you do this in real life.

258
00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:32.000
You should do it in real life, but I'm saying this is what the book wants you to do.

259
00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:36.000
And really, if you really did this, you would, you wouldn't get in trouble.

260
00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:40.000
Your patient would be in good shape.

261
00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:41.000
Okay.

262
00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:49.000
The pH, 7.35, 7.45 is the range, which you told me the other day.

263
00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:54.000
In the last column, the only thing that matters is a pH in the sixes is a D.

264
00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:57.000
Now, I don't mean 7.6.

265
00:16:57.000 --> 00:17:01.000
I'm talking about 6 point something in the sixes.

266
00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:03.000
Now, you notice that I don't...

267
00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:06.000
Do you notice with the creatinine, I didn't go...

268
00:17:06.000 --> 00:17:08.000
I didn't talk about a low creatinine.

269
00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:09.000
Did you notice that?

270
00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:13.000
And in an INR, I didn't talk about a low INR.

271
00:17:13.000 --> 00:17:20.000
And here, I'm not talking about a high pH, because those aren't that clinically significant,

272
00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:22.000
and they're not usually tested at all.

273
00:17:22.000 --> 00:17:27.000
You should have seen, these are the things that are tested over and over and over again.

274
00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:32.000
So rather than have you learned everything, let's focus on what's essential.

275
00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:34.000
Alrighty.

276
00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:35.000
pH in the sixes.

277
00:17:35.000 --> 00:17:36.000
So what do you do?

278
00:17:37.000 --> 00:17:38.000
Is there anything to hold?

279
00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:41.000
I can't think of anything.

280
00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:43.000
What would you assess?

281
00:17:44.000 --> 00:17:45.000
Now, this is interesting.

282
00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:46.000
What do you think you'd assess?

283
00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:49.000
Somebody said respiration.

284
00:17:49.000 --> 00:17:50.000
Somebody said pulse.

285
00:17:52.000 --> 00:17:53.000
There you go.

286
00:17:53.000 --> 00:17:54.000
Love them together.

287
00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:55.000
Check your vital signs.

288
00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:58.000
Because the pH is going in what direction?

289
00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:00.000
Down.

290
00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:03.000
And as the pH goes, so goes your patient.

291
00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:05.000
So the patient is going to be going what?

292
00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:07.000
Down and out.

293
00:18:07.000 --> 00:18:08.000
And you check it.

294
00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:13.000
I mean, with a pH in the sixes, to be totally transparent and honest with you,

295
00:18:13.000 --> 00:18:16.000
you're doing the vitals to make sure they're still alive.

296
00:18:17.000 --> 00:18:21.000
I know that sounds ridiculous, but you really are.

297
00:18:21.000 --> 00:18:23.000
Because I mean, usually when you have a six, you're dead.

298
00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:26.000
So you're just making sure they're alive.

299
00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:28.000
And then is there anything to prepare?

300
00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:31.000
Not really.

301
00:18:31.000 --> 00:18:34.000
Used to be, years ago, because they were severely acidotic,

302
00:18:34.000 --> 00:18:38.000
we would prepare bicarbonate to give bicarb.

303
00:18:38.000 --> 00:18:39.000
But you don't do that anymore.

304
00:18:39.000 --> 00:18:42.000
You don't indiscriminately give bicarbonate like,

305
00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:45.000
if you've ever been taught that, no, you don't do that.

306
00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:48.000
You don't give bicarb anymore to acidotic patients,

307
00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:51.000
because it confuses the whole issue.

308
00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:54.000
The key is to correct this acidosis.

309
00:18:54.000 --> 00:18:58.000
The only way you can correct it is to treat the underlying cause.

310
00:18:59.000 --> 00:19:02.000
And there is nothing a nurse can do to treat the underlying cause.

311
00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:07.000
The physician has to get here, determine the cause, and treat the cause.

312
00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:15.000
So, with a low pH, you have to get the physician in on the case faster

313
00:19:15.000 --> 00:19:18.000
than anything else we'll talk about today.

314
00:19:18.000 --> 00:19:25.000
So, that's why you just assess their vital signs, and you call your doc.

315
00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:30.000
And you skip, hold, and you skip, prepare.

316
00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:33.000
Now, why are you getting the vitals?

317
00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:36.000
Because when you call the doc, what are they going to say?

318
00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:38.000
Is he alive?

319
00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:40.000
You know, aren't he brave?

320
00:19:40.000 --> 00:19:41.000
What's his heart doing?

321
00:19:41.000 --> 00:19:44.000
You know, so get a set of vitals, that's all you need.

322
00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:46.000
Set of vitals, call the doc.

323
00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:48.000
And you stay.

324
00:19:48.000 --> 00:19:50.000
So, who gets the vitals?

325
00:19:50.000 --> 00:19:51.000
You do.

326
00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:53.000
Who calls the doc?

327
00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:57.000
Maybe some, you know, you get up, put on the light or whatever,

328
00:19:57.000 --> 00:20:01.000
and however you communicate to your secretary, unit secretary,

329
00:20:01.000 --> 00:20:03.000
and have them call stat.

330
00:20:03.000 --> 00:20:08.000
Okay, VUN, blood urea nitrogen, VUN.

331
00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:13.000
It has a lot to do with waste nitrogen, waste products in the blood.

332
00:20:13.000 --> 00:20:18.000
Its range is 8 to 25, 8 to 25.

333
00:20:19.000 --> 00:20:23.000
The way I remember that is, VUN spells what English word?

334
00:20:23.000 --> 00:20:24.000
Bun.

335
00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:28.000
Bun, like hamburger buns, hot dog buns.

336
00:20:28.000 --> 00:20:32.000
When you buy hamburger buns or hot dog buns, how many are in the pack?

337
00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:33.000
Eight.

338
00:20:33.000 --> 00:20:34.000
Eight.

339
00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:35.000
There's your eight.

340
00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:36.000
Buns, eight.

341
00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:37.000
Eight to 25.

342
00:20:37.000 --> 00:20:43.000
And if the VUN is elevated, it's no big deal.

343
00:20:43.000 --> 00:20:46.000
All you do is assess them for dehydration.

344
00:20:46.000 --> 00:20:50.000
Assess them for dehydration.

345
00:20:50.000 --> 00:20:55.000
By the way, a little hint, good guessing strategy here.

346
00:20:55.000 --> 00:21:00.000
If they give you an elevated blood value,

347
00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:05.000
and you have no clue what's going on,

348
00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:10.000
and they ask you, for what would you assess them?

349
00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:13.000
Dehydration is a great answer.

350
00:21:14.000 --> 00:21:19.000
Because when you dehydrate, what happens to all blood values?

351
00:21:19.000 --> 00:21:21.000
They go up because of concentration.

352
00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:25.000
So it's just a really good guess to say,

353
00:21:25.000 --> 00:21:27.000
when the BU ends up, oh, I don't know what it is.

354
00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:31.000
Well, pick dehydration, good answer.

355
00:21:31.000 --> 00:21:36.000
You may be wrong, but it was a good answer.

356
00:21:36.000 --> 00:21:41.000
Might not have made the top ten surveys, but you know, it's not bad.

357
00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:47.000
I don't know why I had an extra L on the end of elevated that you know.

358
00:21:47.000 --> 00:21:53.000
Hemoglobin is the next one.

359
00:21:53.000 --> 00:21:57.000
It's 12 to 18.

360
00:21:57.000 --> 00:22:01.000
Now, yes, yeah, I know it's 12 to 16 for women and 14 to 18 for men,

361
00:22:01.000 --> 00:22:04.000
but for humans, it's 12 to 18.

362
00:22:04.000 --> 00:22:10.000
And Bortz doesn't get into lab values versus men versus women,

363
00:22:10.000 --> 00:22:13.000
children versus adults, newborns versus...

364
00:22:13.000 --> 00:22:17.000
They could be on your test, but those are really hard questions,

365
00:22:17.000 --> 00:22:19.000
and you do not have to get those right.

366
00:22:19.000 --> 00:22:24.000
But if you don't know a normal adult hemoglobin, you're in trouble.

367
00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:31.000
If it's 8 to 11, if the hemoglobin is 8 to 11, it's a B,

368
00:22:31.000 --> 00:22:36.000
and you would assess them for what?

369
00:22:36.000 --> 00:22:38.000
Low hemoglobin.

370
00:22:38.000 --> 00:22:42.000
Anemia, a bleeding or malnutrition.

371
00:22:42.000 --> 00:22:47.000
If the hemoglobin, however, falls below an 8, it's a C,

372
00:22:47.000 --> 00:22:51.000
and you must do something.

373
00:22:51.000 --> 00:22:53.000
Okay, well, what are you going to do?

374
00:22:53.000 --> 00:22:56.000
You going to hold anything?

375
00:22:56.000 --> 00:22:58.000
Not that I know of.

376
00:22:58.000 --> 00:23:01.000
You're going to assess for bleeding.

377
00:23:01.000 --> 00:23:06.000
You're going to prepare to administer blood,

378
00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:09.000
and you're going to call the doctor.

379
00:23:09.000 --> 00:23:14.000
Now, LPNs, you would prepare, but you would call...

380
00:23:14.000 --> 00:23:17.000
I'm not on this, but on everything where we've said,

381
00:23:17.000 --> 00:23:21.000
call doctor, you could substitute notify RN.

382
00:23:21.000 --> 00:23:23.000
Do you see what I'm saying?

383
00:23:23.000 --> 00:23:27.000
This could say, hold, assess, prepare, notify RN.

384
00:23:27.000 --> 00:23:31.000
All right?

385
00:23:31.000 --> 00:23:33.000
Bicarb.

386
00:23:33.000 --> 00:23:35.000
The bicarb is what?

387
00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:38.000
2 plus 2 plus 2 equals 6, right?

388
00:23:38.000 --> 00:23:42.000
22 to 26.

389
00:23:42.000 --> 00:23:47.000
And an abnormal bicarb is an A.

390
00:23:47.000 --> 00:23:50.000
We don't care. So what?

391
00:23:50.000 --> 00:23:53.000
You know, I've never, in 37 years of being a nurse,

392
00:23:53.000 --> 00:23:56.000
I have never been yelled at by anybody

393
00:23:56.000 --> 00:24:00.000
because I didn't tell them what somebody's bicarb was.

394
00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:03.000
It's just not a major issue.

395
00:24:03.000 --> 00:24:06.000
Okay, CO2, carbon dioxide.

396
00:24:06.000 --> 00:24:07.000
This is in...

397
00:24:07.000 --> 00:24:11.000
This you are getting from an arterial blood gas, by the way.

398
00:24:11.000 --> 00:24:14.000
The normal range is 35 to 45.

399
00:24:14.000 --> 00:24:23.000
It's the same as the pH just dropping off the 7 point, correct?

400
00:24:23.000 --> 00:24:29.000
In the last column, a CO2 that's high, but in the 50s,

401
00:24:29.000 --> 00:24:37.000
like 51, 57, 56, 59, those are C levels.

402
00:24:37.000 --> 00:24:40.000
That's a C, which means it's critical.

403
00:24:40.000 --> 00:24:42.000
You can't just sit on it.

404
00:24:42.000 --> 00:24:45.000
You got to do something about that. That's not good.

405
00:24:45.000 --> 00:24:48.000
Now, I want to say a disclaimer.

406
00:24:48.000 --> 00:24:51.000
I'm not talking about COPD clients here at all.

407
00:24:51.000 --> 00:24:53.000
That changes everything.

408
00:24:53.000 --> 00:24:58.000
This is for people without COPD.

409
00:24:58.000 --> 00:24:59.000
So what do you do?

410
00:24:59.000 --> 00:25:02.000
Well, is there anything to hold?

411
00:25:02.000 --> 00:25:04.000
A high CO2, anything to hold?

412
00:25:04.000 --> 00:25:08.000
I don't think so. What would you assess?

413
00:25:08.000 --> 00:25:09.000
Respiratory.

414
00:25:09.000 --> 00:25:13.000
Respiratory status.

415
00:25:13.000 --> 00:25:16.000
Now, when it comes to prepare on this one,

416
00:25:16.000 --> 00:25:20.000
there is actually something you can do.

417
00:25:20.000 --> 00:25:23.000
There is actually something you can independently,

418
00:25:23.000 --> 00:25:28.000
as an LPN or an RN do.

419
00:25:28.000 --> 00:25:30.000
What would that be?

420
00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:34.000
Breathing in a bag actually recirculates the CO2,

421
00:25:34.000 --> 00:25:35.000
so we don't want that.

422
00:25:35.000 --> 00:25:38.000
That would be raising the CO2.

423
00:25:38.000 --> 00:25:40.000
What's that?

424
00:25:40.000 --> 00:25:44.000
Oxygen is used for low oxygen, not for high CO2.

425
00:25:44.000 --> 00:25:46.000
Mabel down.

426
00:25:46.000 --> 00:25:47.000
What's that?

427
00:25:47.000 --> 00:25:54.000
I read that if you actually put your thumb in your mouth, it would blow.

428
00:25:54.000 --> 00:25:55.000
Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah.

429
00:25:55.000 --> 00:25:59.000
Or they'll say the candle, this is a candle,

430
00:25:59.000 --> 00:26:00.000
blow out the candle.

431
00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:03.000
That is called pursed lip breathing.

432
00:26:03.000 --> 00:26:05.000
Have you heard of that?

433
00:26:05.000 --> 00:26:11.000
This is what you're going to pick, pursed lip breathing.

434
00:26:11.000 --> 00:26:13.000
Because with pursed lip breathing,

435
00:26:13.000 --> 00:26:16.000
you're prolonging the exhalation.

436
00:26:16.000 --> 00:26:24.000
And if you prolong exhaling, you're getting rid of CO2.

437
00:26:24.000 --> 00:26:29.000
Now, most of the time, that will correct the problem,

438
00:26:29.000 --> 00:26:32.000
so you never have to move to what?

439
00:26:32.000 --> 00:26:35.000
Calm.

440
00:26:35.000 --> 00:26:37.000
How do you know it's going to correct the problem?

441
00:26:37.000 --> 00:26:39.000
They will breathe easier.

442
00:26:39.000 --> 00:26:42.000
Because when they're in the 50s, it's going to be dyspneic.

443
00:26:42.000 --> 00:26:46.000
They'll be dyspneic when their CO2 is in the 50s.

444
00:26:46.000 --> 00:26:50.000
So that will solve that problem.

445
00:26:50.000 --> 00:26:54.000
However, what if the CO2 is in the 60s?

446
00:26:54.000 --> 00:26:56.000
That's a D.

447
00:26:56.000 --> 00:27:02.000
That's one of the criteria for making the diagnosis respiratory failure.

448
00:27:02.000 --> 00:27:04.000
Have you heard of respiratory failure?

449
00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:07.000
Well, how do you know someone's in respiratory failure?

450
00:27:07.000 --> 00:27:15.000
Well, one of the ways you know is the CO2 is 60 and above.

451
00:27:15.000 --> 00:27:20.000
Now, here again, we're not talking about COPDers.

452
00:27:20.000 --> 00:27:22.000
So this is a medical emergency.

453
00:27:22.000 --> 00:27:24.000
This is a super high priority patient.

454
00:27:24.000 --> 00:27:27.000
So you do not what?

455
00:27:27.000 --> 00:27:28.000
Leave the room.

456
00:27:28.000 --> 00:27:30.000
You stay with them.

457
00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:33.000
So is there anything to hold in this case?

458
00:27:33.000 --> 00:27:34.000
Nope.

459
00:27:34.000 --> 00:27:36.000
Assess there?

460
00:27:36.000 --> 00:27:37.000
Respiratory status?

461
00:27:37.000 --> 00:27:40.000
Now, per-slip breathing isn't going to cut it.

462
00:27:40.000 --> 00:27:46.000
Now, I will probably do it with them just to decrease their anxiety

463
00:27:46.000 --> 00:27:48.000
and maybe take the edge off of it.

464
00:27:48.000 --> 00:27:52.000
But I'm going to have to prepare for what two things?

465
00:27:52.000 --> 00:27:54.000
Because they're in respiratory failure.

466
00:27:54.000 --> 00:27:57.000
Intubate and ventilate.

467
00:27:57.000 --> 00:28:02.000
So you prepare to intubate and ventilate.

468
00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:12.000
And then who do you call?

469
00:28:12.000 --> 00:28:13.000
There's two people to call.

470
00:28:13.000 --> 00:28:15.000
Who do you call first?

471
00:28:15.000 --> 00:28:18.000
Respiratory therapy first.

472
00:28:18.000 --> 00:28:20.000
Call respiratory therapy first.

473
00:28:20.000 --> 00:28:27.000
Then call the physician.

474
00:28:27.000 --> 00:28:31.000
But you stay with your patient.

475
00:28:31.000 --> 00:28:32.000
Yes?

476
00:28:32.000 --> 00:28:37.000
Like a code?

477
00:28:37.000 --> 00:28:40.000
A three-code?

478
00:28:40.000 --> 00:28:42.000
Yeah.

479
00:28:42.000 --> 00:28:51.000
On boards, they really don't have anything that's called an emergency response three-code team.

480
00:28:51.000 --> 00:28:52.000
They don't...

481
00:28:52.000 --> 00:28:53.000
That's...

482
00:28:53.000 --> 00:28:55.000
Certain hospitals have that.

483
00:28:55.000 --> 00:28:57.000
Some don't.

484
00:28:57.000 --> 00:29:04.000
And because it's not a universal phenomenon across the nation in every hospital,

485
00:29:04.000 --> 00:29:06.000
they won't even go there.

486
00:29:06.000 --> 00:29:14.000
But I would say if I were you and I had one of those teams at my disposal in a hospital,

487
00:29:14.000 --> 00:29:21.000
yes, at this point I would because this person's going to end up on a vent.

488
00:29:21.000 --> 00:29:24.000
And that's the best that's going to happen.

489
00:29:24.000 --> 00:29:27.000
The worst could happen before.

490
00:29:27.000 --> 00:29:30.000
So, yeah.

491
00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:31.000
All right.

492
00:29:31.000 --> 00:29:36.000
Hematocrit.

493
00:29:36.000 --> 00:29:43.000
Hematocrit is 36 to 54.

494
00:29:43.000 --> 00:29:45.000
Now, I don't remember that.

495
00:29:45.000 --> 00:29:48.000
That's why I had to think for a few seconds before I said it.

496
00:29:48.000 --> 00:29:51.000
I never remember that because that's a clumsy number.

497
00:29:51.000 --> 00:29:54.000
36, 54, you know, it's just sloppy.

498
00:29:54.000 --> 00:29:57.000
Well, it's three times the hemoglobin.

499
00:29:57.000 --> 00:30:00.000
So whatever the hemoglobin is, multiply that by three,

500
00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:04.000
and that's why I had to do the math in my head there for a second before I told you.

501
